.TH COW 1
.SH NAME
cow, spots \- run a command on a copy of this directory, and show what it changed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cow
.I dir
.I cmd
[
.I arg ...
]
.PP
.B spots
.I dir
[
.I base
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cow
runs
.I cmd
in a new name space where
.IR cowfs (4)
serves the current directory, with every change kept in
.IR dir ,
so that the real files do not change.
.I Dir
may be relative to the current directory;
.I cow
makes it if it does not exist.
.I Cow
refuses a
.I dir
that holds anything, or one inside the current directory.
The command's standard output and error are its own:
.I cow
prints only its own errors.
It keeps the changes whatever the command's status, and exits with that status.
.PP
.I Cow
serves only the current directory and what lies below it.
A command that changes to a directory outside it, or that names a file there, works on the real
files.
.PP
.I Spots
prints the changes kept in
.I dir
as a patch for
.IR base ,
or the current directory without one, which must be the tree
.I cow
served.
For each file that the command made, removed, or changed, it prints Git's header,
.BI "diff --git a/" path " b/" path \f1,
then
.B new file mode
or
.B deleted file mode
for a file the command made or removed,
or
.B old mode
and
.B new mode
when the execute bit changed,
and then the unified diff,
against
.B /dev/null
for a file made or removed.
A mode is
.B 100755
when the owner may execute the file, and
.B 100644
otherwise.
The header alone shows an empty file made or removed.
A file changed and then changed back, or made and then removed, does not appear, and
directories appear only through their files.
A file removed and a file made with the same text, which is not empty, show as a rename:
.BI "diff --git a/" old " b/" new \f1,
then the mode lines if the execute bit changed,
.BR "similarity index 100%" ,
.BI "rename from " old \f1,
and
.BI "rename to " new \f1,
with no diff.
Each removed file, in the order of their paths, takes one made file:
of those with its text, the one with the same last element, then the one whose path shares the
longest start with its own, then the first in the order of their paths.
.I Spots
quotes a name holding a control character, a double quote or a backslash as Git does, in
double quotes with C's escapes.
.I Spots
leaves out Git's
.B index
line, so
.B patch -p1
and
.B git apply
both take its output.
It leaves binary files out of the patch, naming each on standard error as
.BI "spots: binary: " path .
.PP
.IR Cowfs
notes the SHA-1 of each file of the real tree as it copies it;
.I spots
warns of a file whose text has changed since then, or that has gone, as applying its patch would
undo that change.
The warning also catches a wrong
.I base
for the files cowfs copied or removed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Work in a shell, keeping its changes apart, then save them as a patch:
.IP
.EX
cow /tmp/e rc
spots /tmp/e | patch
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B rc/bin/cow
and
.B rc/bin/spots
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR diff (1),
.IR cowfs (4)
.SH BUGS
.I Spots
compares the files with APE's
.IR diff ,
which marks no missing newline at the end of a file;
a patch of such a file adds one.
APE's
.I patch
ignores Git's headers, so it makes no empty file and sets no mode.
.PP
A name holding a newline breaks
.IB dir /.cow/files ,
a line each.
Other bytes past ASCII stay as they are in a name, as Git writes them with
.B core.quotepath
off.
.PP
.I Spots
finds a rename only when the text is the same;
a file renamed and changed shows as a removal and a creation.
